Subject: [PATCH] T23 high-frequency stream seamlessness: motion change-guard, no per-change keyframe, gated day/night keyframe, leak-free force_mode
Target: prudynt-t @ f4b32289359d176fb0795d9f3735af0bd9d6d2fd (branch stable).
Stacks on: 0001-0005. Applies with git apply -p1 / patch -p1 AFTER them (verified).

From the high-frequency configuration-robustness review. All changes keep the
active go2rtc video stream seamless under continuous MQTT/remote commands; none
add logging/deps/allocations.

M1 (JsonAPI add_boolk_m): rebuild ONLY on a real change. motion.enabled was the
   one routine remote toggle that set global_restart_video (full T23 rebuild) on
   EVERY send, including a no-op re-send (HA state sync) — cfg->set returns true
   on any valid write, so the stored value is now compared explicitly first.

H1 (IMPEncoder setLiveBitrate/setLiveQp): drop the forced IMP_Encoder_RequestIDR
   per change. An RC/bitrate/QP change keeps the bitstream valid; the next GOP
   IDR refreshes it. The per-change keyframe was a recurring bitrate spike that
   inflated the go2rtc video=copy jitter buffer under continuous reconfig.

H1b (DayNightWorker apply_mode): emit the day/night keyframe ONCE per GENUINE
   colour<->mono cut (new color_cut flag) and only on channels that have a
   viewer — not on every switch and not on unwatched channels. A reconnecting
   subscriber still gets its own IDR on connect.

M8 (Config/JsonAPI/DayNightWorker): force_mode is now an int enum (0=none,
   1=day, 2=night) instead of an atomic<const char*> set via strdup. The config
   layer never frees char values, so the old design leaked one small string per
   force command — unbounded under high-frequency MQTT forcing (~tens of MB/day
   -> eventual watchdog reboot). The int carries no heap, so it cannot leak; the
   persisted force_mode_cfg mirror is now written only on a real change.

